/**

  @page page_peano_localisation Localisation of a solver

Peano allows you to attach data to faces, cells and vertices. It stores these
data everywhere within the domain on each and every resolution level.




## Alter the storage logic

Typically, each data structure has a generator object attached to it which defines how
to translate the logical data model into C++ data structures. These generators
further add MPI logic and so forth, and they create a predicate (guard) if to 
load and store data or not. 
By default, these predicates return true, which means that you hold the data 
everywhere and on each and every resolution level.


A typical code snippet to only store data on the finest grid level then reads
for example

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
self._patch.generator.store_persistent_condition = "not marker.willBeRefined()"
self._patch.generator.load_persistent_condition  = "not marker.hasBeenRefined()"
self._patch.generator.provide_compute_data       = "not marker.hasBeenRefined()"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

which means that the generator makes the marker decide whether to store
data in a cell or not. It is interesting to see that we have three values:
We can decide if we store or load data, but we also can indicate if the 
steps in-between shall provide any compute data. It is important to recognise
that there are 


## Parallelisation

Peano automatically masks out the data outside of the local subdomain of a 
thread or rank.
However, that only works properly with smart pointers.



